Fictional EP Tracklist

Track 1: Fan Opens on the Fifth Impact

Story & Context: The opening track is based on a rehearsal rule in which Adelia keeps the sasando resonator partially closed for the first four complete rhythmic cycles. She plays a narrow midrange figure while Tomé uses only kick, snare, and one short bass pulse. Niko answers every second snare with a muted hand-drum stroke, while Melki keeps a thin seed-shaker line that never lands exactly on the sampler grid.

On the fifth major percussion impact, Adelia opens the palm fan to its widest marked position. The acoustic tone suddenly broadens without any electronic effect. Lara enters immediately afterward with one low bass-flute note, not as harmony but as a shadow beneath the expanded resonance.

The middle section repeats the same harmonic figure with progressively denser sasando arpeggiation while Tomé removes percussion rather than adding it. By the final sixteen bars, the instrumental cascade is at its busiest but the electronic beat has been reduced to isolated kick and clap events.

The track ends with the resonator slowly closing while the last shaker pulse continues alone.

Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Close documentary photograph of a sasando resonator opening like a pale palm fan beneath a timber shelter, five small chalk marks on the floor beside the player, seed shaker and hand drum nearby, compact sampler on a low table. No full sung lyrics; sparse spoken cues limited to “four closed,” “fifth impact,” “open the fan,” and “leave the shaker.”

Track 2: Red Earth Between Two Kicks

Story & Context: Tomé builds the second track around a pair of heavy kicks separated by a conspicuously empty space. Adelia places a descending sasando run entirely inside that gap, beginning on a high string and ending on a low open fifth just before the second kick. The gesture becomes the central grammar of the piece: electronic force establishes boundaries, while the acoustic instrument occupies the interior.

Lara avoids pitched flute for the first half and contributes breath-only attacks after every third descending run. Niko uses palm-muted drum strokes to reinforce the first kick but deliberately ignores the second. Melki answers instead with ankle rattles and a heel stamp, making the same electronic pair feel different on alternate cycles.

Halfway through, Tomé removes the first kick and leaves only the second. The sasando run remains unchanged, so it suddenly feels as if the phrase begins in midair. When the missing kick returns eight cycles later, its reappearance feels heavier despite no change in level.

The final cycle contains no electronic kick at all. Two low sasando notes and one heel stamp imply the absent pair.

Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Red-brown earth floor marked by two dark heel impressions with a wide empty space between them, sasando strings cutting diagonally through the frame, bass flute and seed rattles resting nearby, strong dry-season shadows. Spoken fragments limited to “between two kicks,” “leave the first,” “eight cycles,” and “the ground remembers.”

Track 3: Wind Crosses the Palm Before the Drop

Story & Context: The EP closer begins during a period of unusually strong afternoon wind. Instead of filtering the environmental sound, the band allows moving palm leaves around the shelter to remain in the room microphones. Adelia plays the slowest sasando pattern on the record: wide open fifths and suspended tones separated by large spaces. Tomé keeps the 144 BPM tempo but removes most of the usual kuduro density, leaving terse kicks, one dry snare, and short bass pulses.

Lara develops a breath-heavy figure that begins each time a gust becomes audible. Niko and Melki use almost no continuous percussion, only isolated drum slaps, shaker bursts, and foot stamps. The arrangement gradually teaches the listener to expect a full rhythmic drop, but when the anticipated transition arrives, Tomé cuts every electronic element instead.

For one full sixteen-bar passage, only sasando, wind, bass flute, and human percussion remain. Adelia accelerates the string cascade during the electronic absence, proving that the track can reach its highest kinetic state without the programmed beat.

The electronics return only for the final eight bars: one clipped kick pattern, no sub-bass, and a dry clap beneath the closing sasando chord. The last audible sound is wind moving across the palm resonator after every player has stopped.

